From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:12:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 055/342] dwc2: isochronous transfers for full speed devices
 behind the hub

USB audio devices (usb_audio) need isochronous endpoints, which the DWC2
host driver rejected. On the ODROID-C2 every port is behind the GL852G
high speed hub, so a full speed device's isochronous packets go through
the hub's transaction translator as split transactions, one packet per
1 ms frame:
* OUT: start-splits only, in consecutive microframes, 188 bytes each
  ("all", or "begin" ... "end"): 192 bytes per frame at 48 kHz stereo
  take two;
* IN: a start-split, then complete-splits until one returns the data;
  pieces handed over as MDATA are put together again.

Each stream has a host channel of its own. A kernel timer fires in the
microframe before a packet's start microframe and arms the channel; the
halt interrupt arms the following parts in the next microframes. A packet
that cannot start in time is skipped, so each packet keeps its frame. OUT
packets start in microframe 0, IN packets in microframe 2: a USB headset
adapter's microphone answered a third of IN tokens at the very start of
its frame with zero-length packets.

The data waits in 32-bit DMA bounce slots; a worker thread finishes the
transfers. Nothing is printed while a stream runs (serial debug output
keeps interrupts off for milliseconds, which cost packets); a summary
follows when it stops. The register definitions moved to dwc2_hardware.h.

On the ODROID-C2 with a USB-C headset adapter (AB13X, 48 kHz): playback
and microphone both at exactly 48000 frames/s, through usb_audio directly
and through the media_server (MediaPlayer); in two minutes 120036 OUT
packets without a loss or a split gap, 12 of 120080 IN packets lost.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
